# {{FACTORY_NAME}}

A [jigs](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs) factory: the pipelines this
machine runs, the bindings they run against, and the service that hosts them.

## Layout

- `jigs.config.ts` — the pipelines this factory hosts, keyed by the name
  `jigs run` takes. `jigs build` generates the service entry from it.
- `pipelines/` — the pipeline bodies. Their paths are load-bearing: the
  workflow ids the SDK records are derived from them, and a rename orphans
  every parked run.
- `steps/jigs.ts` — this factory's `"use step"` wrappers around the steps jigs
  ships, and the jigs wired on top of them. Every step a pipeline calls comes
  from here, and each step id is this path plus the function's name, so both
  are load-bearing exactly as `pipelines/` is. `jigs init` wrote it once and
  never rewrites it; a step jigs grows later gets its wrapper added by hand.
- `steps/describe-pr.ts` — the one review-loop dep jigs ships no default for:
  the title and body a pull request opens with. Deterministic as scaffolded;
  compose it over the `agent` jig and the `readDiff` wrapper to ask an agent.
- `jigs.config.test.ts` — what the factory declares, and the ids the last
  build emitted.
- `jigs.yml` — this factory's service and dashboard ports and its bindings.
- `docker-compose.yml` — this factory's own Postgres World.

## Setup

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # then fill in LINEAR_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN
jigs up                   # install, World, bootstrap, build, start, doctor
jigs bind git@github.com:owner/repo.git
jigs service restart      # the service clones every binding when it starts
```

The service listens on `:{{SERVICE_PORT}}`, its dashboard on
`:{{DASHBOARD_PORT}}`, its World on `:{{POSTGRES_PORT}}`.

## Pipelines

- `ship` — a ticket to a merged pull request: claim the ticket, snapshot it,
  review it into a brief, cut a worktree, then the review loop (implement ⇄
  review → PR → gate → merge → teardown). Takes the ticket as `ABC-123` or its
  uuid, `binding` names the repo, and `merge` is `jigs` (squash-merge on
  approval) or `human` (a person presses merge).

## Running a pipeline

```sh
jigs run ship --input ticket=ABC-123 --input binding=repo --input merge=human
jigs ps
jigs logs <run>
jigs cancel <run>
```

`<run>` is a run id, a unique prefix of one, or the ticket the run claimed.
Runs outlive the terminal that launched them: the service owns them.

Every launch preflights first, so an empty `.env` refuses the run rather than
creating one that fails later. `jigs doctor` runs the same checks without
launching anything.

## Development

```sh
pnpm typecheck
jigs build
pnpm test                 # jigs.config.ts's assertions, and the built ids
```

`pnpm test` reads the workflow and step ids out of the last build's
`.output/server/index.mjs`, so build first.

## Adding a pipeline

New file in `pipelines/`, exporting a `<name>Inputs` zod schema and a
`<name>Pipeline` function whose body opens with `"use workflow"`. Register it
in `jigs.config.ts` under the name `jigs run` should take, then `jigs build`,
and give the new `workflow//./pipelines/<file>//<fn>` id its line in
`WORKFLOW_IDS` in `jigs.config.test.ts`.

A pipeline imports its steps from `../steps/jigs.ts`, never from
`@salimhamed/jigs-service`: a step taken from the package would be addressed
by the package's version, and every jigs upgrade would rename it. A pipeline
may also declare `"use step"` functions in its own file; their ids come out as
`step//./pipelines/<file>//<fn>`, so those names are load-bearing too, and
each gets its line in `STEP_IDS`.

The jigs — `agent`, `ask`, `needsHuman`, `gate`, `agentOrHalt`,
`ticketReview`, `reviewLoop` — are compositions over those steps and carry no
id. `steps/jigs.ts` exports them already wired to this factory's steps; a
pipeline that must swap one dep spreads `reviewLoopDeps` and overrides the
entry.

## Upgrading jigs

Both packages release in lockstep, so move them together, then take the
factory back through `jigs up`:

```sh
pnpm update @salimhamed/jigs@<version> @salimhamed/jigs-service@<version>
jigs up
```

Installing needs a token that can read GitHub Packages in `~/.npmrc`; the
`.npmrc` here only routes the scope.

A release that grows a step needs a wrapper in `steps/jigs.ts` and a line in
`STEP_IDS`; the typecheck names the gap. A release that adds a runtime
dependency needs it in `package.json` too — compare against `jigs init`'s
template.
